sections.body_md:
In India, nationalism grew together with the **anti-colonial movement**: people of different classes discovered a common opponent in colonial rule, but each group felt that rule differently.

**The First World War (1914–18)** left India poorer and angrier:
- defence spending was met with **war loans**, higher **customs duties** and a new **income tax**;
- **prices roughly doubled** between 1913 and 1918;
- villages were pressed into supplying soldiers through **forced recruitment**;
- crops failed in 1918–19 and 1920–21, and an **influenza epidemic** followed; millions died of famine and disease.

**Satyagraha.** Gandhiji returned from South Africa in **January 1915**. His method insisted on **truth** and **non-violence**: a person with a just cause need not use force, and could win by appealing to the **conscience** of the oppressor. He tried it out in three local struggles:
- **Champaran, Bihar (1917)** — peasants against the oppressive **indigo** plantation system;
- **Kheda, Gujarat (1917–18)** — peasants hit by **crop failure and plague** asked for relief from revenue collection;
- **Ahmedabad (1918)** — a satyagraha of **cotton mill workers**.

questions.stem_md:
Mark and name these two places of the national movement on an outline map of India:

**A.** the Gujarat district whose peasants, ruined by a failed harvest and an outbreak of plague, sought remission of land revenue in 1917–18;

**B.** the seaside village of southern Gujarat that the salt marchers reached after walking for 24 days.

On the outline map of India provided, show with a dot and name:

**A.** the city of central India where the annual Congress session of December 1920 adopted the programme of non-cooperation;

**B.** the southern port city that hosted the Congress session of 1927.

Locate and label on an outline political map of India:

**A.** the place where Gandhiji's inquiry into the hardships of indigo cultivators became his first satyagraha in India;

**B.** the city where the Congress, meeting in December 1927, resolved to boycott the all-British Simon Commission.

Name and mark the following on the outline political map of India:

**A.** the village on the Arabian Sea coast where the Civil Disobedience Movement began when Gandhiji made salt from sea water;

**B.** the region at the foot of the Himalaya in Bihar where planters had made peasants grow indigo on part of their land.
